---
scope: src/tools/info
description: >
  Lightweight boot-time tool that reports both AIDE artifact staleness (local versions vs. shipped manifest) and brain precondition state (whether an obsidian MCP entry is configured and its vault directory exists) so the orchestrator can branch on each.
intent: >
  aide_info is the passive boot-time precondition reporter the orchestrator
  calls once during its boot sequence. It surfaces two independent concerns
  in a single structured response. First, the existing staleness concern:
  it reads the host's .aide/versions.json from disk, compares each
  artifact's sourceCommit against the canonical manifest shipped inside
  the npm package, and returns which artifacts are outdated plus the
  server's npm version. Second, the brain-precondition concern: it reads
  the host's framework-specific MCP config (the same config path used by
  wireMcp during init), looks for an obsidian-style server entry, and if
  one exists resolves its configured vault path and stats the directory.
  The brain state is returned as a tagged union — status "ok" when an
  entry exists and the vault directory resolves, "no-mcp-entry" when no
  obsidian entry is present (or the MCP config file itself is missing or
  malformed), and "invalid-path" when an entry exists but the configured
  vault directory does not exist on disk — alongside the configured
  vaultPath (or null) so the orchestrator can compose a precise message.
  The tool stays passive: it reports state, it never blocks, warns, or
  provisions anything. The orchestrator treats staleness as a soft
  notification and brain state as a hard gate — that policy lives in the
  orchestrator, not here. Missing MCP config, malformed MCP config, and
  missing vault paths are all normal pre-init states and must resolve to
  structured states, never thrown errors. The name stays aide_info (not
  aide_versions or aide_preconditions) to leave room for additional
  boot-time metadata in the future without renaming.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "Reads the host project's .aide/versions.json from disk and compares each artifact's sourceCommit against the shipped canonical manifest. Returns `{ serverVersion, outdated }` where outdated is an array of artifact keys whose sourceCommit differs (including artifacts present in canonical but missing locally)."
    - The response is small enough to add negligible cost to every orchestrator boot sequence. Only the list of outdated artifact keys, the server version, the brain-state object, and nothing else.
    - "The tool returns structured JSON, not a pre-formatted message. The orchestrator composes its own user-facing notification from the data."
    - "When the local .aide/versions.json is missing or unreadable, the tool returns `outdated: []` silently — it does not error or warn. Old installs that predate version tracking are handled gracefully."
    - "Reads the host's framework-specific MCP config file and reports whether an obsidian-style MCP server entry is configured, returning the result as part of a structured brain-state object."
    - "When an obsidian MCP entry is configured, extracts the vault directory path from the entry and stats it, reporting whether the directory exists on disk alongside the configured path."
    - "Returns brain state as a tagged union the orchestrator can branch on — shape `{ status: 'ok' | 'no-mcp-entry' | 'invalid-path', vaultPath: string | null }` — not as a pre-formatted message."
    - "Missing MCP config file, malformed MCP config, and a missing obsidian entry all resolve to `status: 'no-mcp-entry'` with `vaultPath: null` — structured states, never thrown errors."
    - "An obsidian entry whose configured vault path does not resolve to an existing directory resolves to `status: 'invalid-path'` with `vaultPath` set to the configured path so the orchestrator can tell the user exactly which path failed."
    - "Brain state and staleness are returned as two independent top-level fields in the response. The orchestrator can read and branch on each without them interfering."
  undesired:
    - A tool that requires the agent to read the local versions file and pass it in. The tool has the project root and reads the file itself.
    - A tool that triggers or recommends an upgrade. This is a passive notification surface; the orchestrator decides what to say.
    - A response that includes the full versions manifest. Only the outdated keys, server version, and brain-state object are needed.
    - A tool that throws or errors when the brain is simply not configured. Missing MCP config, missing obsidian entry, and missing vault directory are all normal pre-init states and must resolve to structured brain-state values, not exceptions.
    - A tool that returns a pre-formatted "brain missing" message instead of structured state. Message composition belongs to the orchestrator, not this tool.
    - "A tool that attempts to provision the brain, scaffold a vault, or write to the host's MCP config file. This tool is strictly read-only — detection and reporting only, no mutation."
    - "A tool that surfaces brain state as part of the `outdated` array. Staleness is a soft notification and brain state is a hard precondition gate — they are distinct concerns and must be returned as distinct fields."
    - "A tool that conflates 'no obsidian entry configured' with 'obsidian entry configured but vault path missing'. The orchestrator needs to distinguish these to pick the right remediation — run npx aidemd-mcp init to set one up vs. fix the path in the existing entry."
---

## Context

The `/aide` orchestrator boots on every invocation by firing a fixed set of
reads in parallel — the canonical methodology docs, the intent tree, and
this tool. Two preconditions must be checked before any pipeline work runs,
and they are structurally different. Staleness — whether the host's
installed AIDE artifacts (docs, commands, agents, skills) match the
canonical manifest shipped with this npm package — is a soft signal: if
something drifted, the user should know, but the pipeline can still run.
Brain availability — whether the host has an obsidian-style MCP server
configured and whether its vault directory exists on disk — is a hard
gate: the research and QA phases persist domain findings and retros into
that vault, and without a resolvable vault those phases silently lose
their outputs. The orchestrator must branch differently on each: notify
on staleness, halt on brain-missing.

Both concerns are detectable from read-only disk inspection and both
need to be known at the same instant — the boot sequence. Giving them
separate tools would double the boot-time MCP call count and force each
detector to re-resolve the same host context (project root, detected
framework, MCP config path) independently. Collapsing them into a single
passive reporter lets the tool walk the MCP config file exactly once and
produce both findings from one pass. The orchestrator still branches
separately on each, because the data is returned as two independent
fields, not fused into a single verdict.

Passive is the load-bearing word. Missing MCP config, malformed MCP
config, missing obsidian entry, and missing vault directory are all
normal pre-init states — the tool gets called on cold projects before
`npx aidemd-mcp init` has ever run. A detector that throws on these states would
turn every first-ever orchestrator boot into an error. A detector that
auto-provisions them would violate the init surface's contract that
brain-vault location is an inherent user decision. The tool reports;
the orchestrator decides; `npx aidemd-mcp init` remediates.

## Strategy

**Return `outdated` and `brain` as two independent top-level fields, not
a single fused verdict.** The orchestrator applies different policies to
each — staleness is a soft notification ("N artifacts outdated — run
`/aide:upgrade`"), brain state is a hard precondition gate ("brain not
configured — run `npx aidemd-mcp init` before continuing"). Fusing them into one
boolean or one status enum forces the orchestrator to unpack what the
tool already knew; keeping them independent means each branch in the
orchestrator reads only the field it cares about. This traces to the
desired outcome that brain state and staleness are returned as two
independent top-level fields so the orchestrator can branch on each
without interference.

**Staleness detection stays exactly as today: read
`.aide/versions.json`, compare each artifact's `sourceCommit` against
the canonical manifest, return `outdated[]` and `serverVersion`.** The
canonical manifest ships inside the npm package (read via
`readVersionsManifest` in `src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/`) so the
comparison is always local — no network. When the host's
`.aide/versions.json` is missing or unreadable (an old install predating
version tracking, or a project that never ran init), the tool returns
`outdated: []` silently. A pre-init project is not "stale" — it simply
has nothing to compare — and surfacing a phantom warning would train
the user to ignore the notification.

**Brain detection reuses `detectFramework` from
`src/tools/init/detectFramework/` to resolve the framework-specific MCP
config path.** The init tree already encodes the per-framework mapping
(`.mcp.json` for Claude Code, `.cursor/mcp.json` for Cursor, etc.) in
`FRAMEWORK_CONFIGS`. Duplicating that mapping inside the info tool
creates a second source of truth that will silently drift when a new
framework is added. Calling `detectFramework` once at the top of the
info flow gives the tool the same `mcpConfigPath` that `wireMcp` and
`provisionBrain` write into — so detection and provisioning agree by
construction.

**Brain state is returned as a tagged union — `{ status: 'ok' |
'no-mcp-entry' | 'invalid-path', vaultPath: string | null }` — not a
boolean.** A boolean collapses two cases the orchestrator must handle
differently: "no obsidian entry configured at all" remediates with
`npx aidemd-mcp init` (which runs the vault-path interview and adds a fresh
entry), while "obsidian entry configured but vault path does not
resolve" remediates by fixing the path in the existing entry (the user
moved or deleted the folder). The tagged union preserves the
remediation signal the orchestrator needs. This traces to the undesired
outcome that forbids conflating these two cases.

**Missing MCP config, malformed MCP config, and missing obsidian entry
all collapse to `status: 'no-mcp-entry'` with `vaultPath: null`.** All
three conditions share the same remediation path: run `npx aidemd-mcp init` to
configure the brain end-to-end. The orchestrator does not need separate
branches for "the JSON was bad" vs "the key was missing" because the
user's next action is identical. Collapsing them to one tagged state
keeps the orchestrator's boot-branch logic narrow and guarantees the
tool never throws — malformed JSON is caught and mapped, not rethrown.
This traces to the desired outcome that these three cases all resolve
to `no-mcp-entry` as structured states, never thrown errors.

**`status: 'invalid-path'` stays distinct from `no-mcp-entry` precisely
because the remediation diverges.** When an obsidian entry exists but
the configured vault directory does not resolve on disk, the user does
not need a fresh `npx aidemd-mcp init` interview — they need to either restore
the folder or point the existing entry at its new location.
Distinguishing this case lets the orchestrator compose a message that
names the exact configured path that failed, which a generic
"brain missing" message cannot do. The tagged union's `vaultPath` field
is populated with the configured (but unresolvable) path in this case,
never in the `no-mcp-entry` case — so the orchestrator can always trust
the field's presence as a signal of which state it is in.

**No framework detected collapses trivially to `no-mcp-entry`.** When
`detectFramework` defaults to Claude Code on a cold project (no
framework markers on disk), the subsequent MCP config read finds
nothing, and the tool returns `no-mcp-entry`. The default-to-Claude
branch is already the right behavior for the init flow — reusing it
here means the info tool inherits the same "cold project" semantics
without a second code path.

**Brain-entry detection mirrors the dual-key existence check from
`wireMcp`.** `wireMcp` uses `"aide" in servers || "aidemd-mcp" in
servers` for the aide entry; the obsidian equivalent here is the
`"obsidian"` key check used in `provisionBrain`. Matching those exact
key checks guarantees the info tool sees an entry as present if and
only if `provisionBrain` would skip creating one — so the orchestrator
cannot be told "brain is configured" for an entry that `aide_init`
would turn around and add a duplicate of. This traces to the desired
outcome that an entry is considered configured when and only when
`provisionBrain`'s dual-key check would consider it present.

**Vault path extraction reads from the obsidian entry's `args` array.**
The obsidian entry constructed by `provisionBrain` stores the vault
path as the final positional argument (after the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
package name, plus the `cmd /c` and `npx` prefix on Windows). The
detector pulls the last element of `args` as the candidate path. If
the entry is missing, malformed, the path element is absent, or its
last element is not a string, the tool returns `no-mcp-entry` — the
entry shape is broken and the only remediation is to re-run
`npx aidemd-mcp init`. One case is treated differently: if the last
element is an empty string, the tool returns `invalid-path` with
`vaultPath: ""`. This is the deliberate cold-install state written by
the CLI — the entry shell is correctly shaped but the vault path has
not been filled in yet, and the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow
(`AskUserQuestion`) is what prompts the user to supply it. Routing the
empty-string case to `no-mcp-entry` would send the user back to
`npx aidemd-mcp init` (which they just ran), creating a loop.
`invalid-path` with an empty `vaultPath` is the signal the orchestrator
keys on to fire the inline-recovery branch instead.

**Vault-directory existence is checked with a single `stat`/`access`
call.** The tool is passive: it does not walk the vault, inspect its
contents, verify the `.obsidian/` marker, or validate the AIDE
directory scaffolding. A single "does this directory exist on disk"
check is the minimum signal that distinguishes `ok` from
`invalid-path`, and any richer check creates more cases the tool has
to resolve and the orchestrator has to branch on. `provisionBrain` is
responsible for vault contents; info is responsible for "does the
path resolve at all".

**The tool writes nothing, provisions nothing, and pre-formats
nothing.** Even when it detects a fully broken brain state, it returns
structured data and exits. Message composition belongs to the
orchestrator (which tailors its wording to the user's context), and
remediation belongs to `npx aidemd-mcp init`. This traces directly to the
undesired outcomes that forbid provisioning, pre-formatted messages,
and mutation as side effects of the detection flow.

## Good examples

A fully set-up project, brain configured and vault on disk, all
artifacts current:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "ok",
    "vaultPath": "/home/user/vault"
  }
}
```

A project whose brain is healthy but whose commands drifted after a
recent server upgrade:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": ["commands/aide/aide", "commands/aide/plan"],
  "brain": {
    "status": "ok",
    "vaultPath": "/home/user/vault"
  }
}
```

A cold project where `npx aidemd-mcp init` has never run — no MCP config file,
no versions file, nothing to warn about, but the brain is also
unconfigured:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "no-mcp-entry",
    "vaultPath": null
  }
}
```

A project that was fully initialized months ago, but the user moved
their vault directory to a new disk and never updated the MCP config
— obsidian entry still present, configured path no longer resolves:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "invalid-path",
    "vaultPath": "/old/moved/path"
  }
}
```

The orchestrator reads the configured path out of `vaultPath` and can
tell the user exactly which path failed — something a boolean
`brainConfigured: false` could never express.

## Bad examples

A tool that throws when `.mcp.json` is missing:

```
Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '.mcp.json'
    at aide_info ...
```

Every first-ever orchestrator boot on a cold project crashes here. The
detection flow must absorb missing files as a legitimate pre-init state
and map them to `no-mcp-entry`, never bubble them as exceptions.

A tool that returns a pre-formatted message instead of structured
state:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": "Brain is missing — run npx aidemd-mcp init to set it up."
}
```

The orchestrator cannot compose a message tailored to the user's
context, cannot distinguish between no-entry and invalid-path, and is
reduced to parroting a string the tool decided on. Message composition
is the orchestrator's job.

A tool that collapses `no-mcp-entry` and `invalid-path` into a single
boolean:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "configured": false
  }
}
```

The orchestrator now has no way to recommend the right remediation. A
user whose vault was moved is told to run `npx aidemd-mcp init` (which would
add a duplicate entry), and a user with no entry at all is told the
same thing — with no vault path surfaced either way.

A tool that conflates brain state into the `outdated` array:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": ["commands/aide/aide", "brain"],
  "brain": null
}
```

Staleness and brain availability have different severities — one is a
soft notification, the other is a hard gate. Folding them together
forces the orchestrator to scan the `outdated` array for the magic
`"brain"` sentinel and forks the gate policy inside the staleness
branch. Two fields at the top level avoids this.

A tool that repairs what it finds:

```
aide_info called → detected missing obsidian entry →
merged obsidian entry into .mcp.json → returned brain: { status: 'ok' }
```

The tool is supposed to be read-only. Writing to `.mcp.json` as a side
effect of a boot-time detection call means every orchestrator boot
becomes a mutation, and the brain-vault-location decision — which the
init surface guarantees is an inherent user decision resolved through
interview — gets silently decided by a detector. This is the failure
mode the init spec's "no silent fallback" rule exists to prevent.

## References

- `src/tools/info/index.ts` -- current staleness-only implementation
  establishing the `.aide/versions.json` read, `sourceCommit`
  comparison against the canonical manifest, and silent-empty-array
  fallback when the file is missing — inherited unchanged by the new
  response shape.
- `src/tools/init/detectFramework/index.ts` -- `FRAMEWORK_CONFIGS` map
  provides the per-framework `mcpConfigPath` (`.mcp.json` for Claude
  Code, `.cursor/mcp.json` for Cursor, etc.) the brain detector needs
  to locate the config file, and the default-to-Claude branch defines
  the cold-project behavior.
- `src/tools/init/wireMcp/index.ts` -- dual-key existence check
  (`"aide" in servers || "aidemd-mcp" in servers`) establishes the
  entry-presence contract the obsidian detector mirrors with
  `"obsidian" in servers`; malformed-JSON branch demonstrates the
  pattern of mapping parse failures to a structured state rather than
  throwing.
- `src/tools/init/provisionBrain/index.ts` -- `buildObsidianMcpStep`
  defines the exact obsidian key (`"obsidian"`) and entry shape
  (positional vault-path arg in `args`) the detector must recognize;
  aligns the info-side detection with the init-side provisioning so
  the two sides agree on what "entry present" means.
- `src/tools/init/.aide` -- establishes that brain-vault location is
  an inherent user decision always resolved through the `npx aidemd-mcp init`
  interview; confirms the info tool must not silently default or
  fallback to a vault path, only report whether one is configured.
- `src/cli/init/.aide` -- confirms that the CLI defers brain-vault
  provisioning entirely to the `npx aidemd-mcp init` run, so a
  project bootstrapped via `npx` lands in `no-mcp-entry` state and
  the info tool must resolve that cleanly without erroring.
- `.claude/commands/aide.md` -- MANDATORY BOOT SEQUENCE step 6 calls
  `aide_info` and currently only branches on `outdated`; documents
  the policy difference (staleness = soft notification, brain state
  will be a hard gate) that drives keeping the two fields
  independent.
- `.aide/intent.aide` -- the parent intent establishes that AIDE is
  an opinionated pipeline with a brain dependency and that the server
  is a renderer, not a decision-maker; the info tool's passive-
  reporter posture inherits directly from this.
